---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: genesis-docs-sibling-homes
purpose: >
  The genesis docs root, the protocol's interpretability layer. Every doc has one home, chosen by
  its kind and status (PLACEMENT.md): canonical architecture and history under
  content/elohim-protocol/, and in-flight specs, plans and sprint results under superpowers/. The
  recurring drift here is a new sibling home born beside those, often parallel to one that
  already exists.
rules:
  - id: new-sibling-home-declares-itself
    class: dispatch
    when: { new: true }
    require-sibling: ".epr-meta"
    parameters:
      dispatch-agent: librarian
      dispatch-prompt: >
        A write is creating a new directory under genesis/docs/. Check whether an existing home
        already holds this kind of document (genesis/docs/PLACEMENT.md). Canonical architecture
        lives in content/elohim-protocol/architecture/, research in genesis/research/, and sprint
        results and shift journals in superpowers/sprints/. Report ROUTE (name the existing home) or
        KEEP (a genuinely new kind of home, which needs its own .epr-meta declaring its purpose),
        with the shortest supporting reason. Do not create, move, or rewrite files; report to the
        editing agent and let development continue.
    why: >
      New directories keep appearing under genesis/docs/ beside the governed homes, several of
      them parallel to an existing one: architecture/ beside content/elohim-protocol/architecture/,
      research/ beside genesis/research/, integration/ beside integrations/, and shifts/ beside
      superpowers/sprints/. Put the document in the existing home for its kind (PLACEMENT.md). If
      it really is a new kind of home, write its .epr-meta first, stating what belongs there, so
      the directory is born governed.
---
# genesis/docs — the placement root

`PLACEMENT.md` is the contract: one home per doc, chosen by kind and status. The two governed
design surfaces carry `root: true` (`superpowers/` and `content/elohim-protocol/`), so this
manifest never reaches them. Everything else under `genesis/docs/` inherits the one rule here.

The rule is a structural guardrail, not a teaching rule. It fires whenever a write creates a new
directory without that directory's own `.epr-meta`, at any depth under this root, and asks a
background librarian whether an existing home already fits. Writes into existing directories are
untouched. The legacy `plans/` and `specs/` manifests below still route their own births to
`superpowers/`.
